1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:11,850
Sebastian Crane had never lost at anything
that could be measured.

2
00:00:12,419 --> 00:00:18,206
He had built his first company at nineteen, sold it
at twenty-three, and founded Lumen

3
00:00:18,226 --> 00:00:24,417
at twenty-five, in a rented house in Seattle with four
friends and a line of credit. Fifteen

4
00:00:24,437 --> 00:00:30,223
years later, Lumen ran a third of the world's cloud
computing, and trained the largest

5
00:00:30,243 --> 00:00:36,231
artificial intelligence systems on Earth, and was
worth two trillion dollars. Crane owned

6
00:00:36,251 --> 00:00:42,172
ten percent of it. He wore black T-shirts and a watch
that tracked his sleep, and he did

7
00:00:42,192 --> 00:00:45,615
not believe in anything that could
not be modelled.

8
00:00:46,185 --> 00:00:52,048
In the spring of twenty thirty-three, he became
obsessed with Albert Wesker. It was not

9
00:00:52,068 --> 00:00:56,308
personal. Crane did not really do
personal. It was the numbers.

10
00:00:56,878 --> 00:01:02,434
Umbrella had gone from nothing to the most valuable
company on Earth in six and a half

11
00:01:02,454 --> 00:01:08,204
years. Nothing had ever done that. Not Lumen, not
Apple, not Standard Oil. And when Crane

12
00:01:08,224 --> 00:01:13,715
had asked his own systems why — when he had fed
them every public record Umbrella had

13
00:01:13,735 --> 00:01:19,226
ever produced, every patent, every filing, every
trade, every speech, every second of

14
00:01:19,246 --> 00:01:24,802
every video — his systems had come back with an
answer that he did not understand, and

15
00:01:24,822 --> 00:01:26,747
could not stop thinking about.

16
00:01:28,816 --> 00:01:34,681
The system was called Oracle. It was the most
powerful model Lumen had ever built, and

17
00:01:34,701 --> 00:01:40,840
Crane had spent fifteen billion dollars teaching it to
predict how companies would behave.

18
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:47,203
It was very, very good at it. It could predict, with
uncanny accuracy, what a chief executive

19
00:01:47,223 --> 00:01:53,225
would do next — because chief executives, Oracle had
learned, were human, and humans are

20
00:01:53,245 --> 00:01:53,841
patterns.

21
00:01:54,411 --> 00:01:56,564
It could not predict Albert Wesker.

22
00:01:57,254 --> 00:02:02,500
Subject decisions are not consistent with a human
utility function. Subject decisions

23
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:08,014
are consistent with optimisation against an external
reward signal that is not observable

24
00:02:08,034 --> 00:02:13,466
in public data. Timing of subject actions correlates
with reward events at a probability

25
00:02:13,486 --> 00:02:18,857
exceeding chance by a factor of ten to the eleventh.
Sixty-three patent filings exhibit

26
00:02:18,877 --> 00:02:24,247
discontinuous capability gain with no antecedents.
Conclusion: subject has access to an

27
00:02:24,267 --> 00:02:26,726
optimiser. Nature of optimiser: unknown.

28
00:02:28,746 --> 00:02:33,301
Crane read that paragraph perhaps a thousand
times over the next month.

29
00:02:33,872 --> 00:02:40,084
An external reward signal. An optimiser. Something was
paying Albert Wesker for his decisions

30
00:02:40,104 --> 00:02:45,914
— something invisible, something that could not be
seen in any data — and paying him in

31
00:02:45,934 --> 00:02:51,745
capabilities that appeared from nowhere. Oracle had
not said what. Oracle could not say

32
00:02:51,765 --> 00:02:57,508
what. But it had described, with the flat precision
of a machine, the shape of a thing

33
00:02:57,528 --> 00:02:58,982
that should not exist.

34
00:02:59,552 --> 00:03:04,221
It had described, although Sebastian Crane
would never know it, the System.

35
00:03:06,290 --> 00:03:10,486
And Crane — being Crane — did not feel
afraid. He felt envy.

36
00:03:12,556 --> 00:03:13,997
He tried to build one.

37
00:03:14,568 --> 00:03:21,113
It was the most expensive secret project in the history
of his company. Four hundred engineers,

38
00:03:21,133 --> 00:03:27,057
a data centre in Oregon, and a single goal: to
build, for Sebastian Crane, what Oracle

39
00:03:27,077 --> 00:03:33,415
believed Albert Wesker had. An artificial optimiser
that would watch Crane's every decision,

40
00:03:33,435 --> 00:03:39,704
reward the good ones, and — somehow, from the sum of
all human knowledge in Lumen's servers

41
00:03:39,724 --> 00:03:42,814
— hand him capabilities that
nobody else had.

42
00:03:43,384 --> 00:03:49,285
It did not work. It could not work. It produced very
good advice, and several patents,

43
00:03:49,305 --> 00:03:55,481
and a great deal of analysis. But it did not produce a
single orphan idea — a single thing

44
00:03:55,501 --> 00:04:01,333
that had not been, somewhere, in the data it had
learned from. Everything it gave him

45
00:04:01,353 --> 00:04:02,159
had parents.

46
00:04:02,729 --> 00:04:08,444
The thing that Albert Wesker had did not come from
the data. It came from outside. And

47
00:04:08,464 --> 00:04:14,246
Sebastian Crane, who did not believe in anything
that could not be modelled, spent four

48
00:04:14,266 --> 00:04:19,981
months and nine billion dollars proving to himself
that it could not be modelled — and

49
00:04:20,001 --> 00:04:22,849
then refused to believe what
he had proved.

50
00:04:24,918 --> 00:04:30,841
They met in June, on a rooftop in San Francisco,
after a conference at which they had

51
00:04:30,861 --> 00:04:34,617
both been scheduled to speak and
only one of them had.

52
00:04:35,187 --> 00:04:40,613
Crane walked straight up to him at the railing,
without introduction, and without small

53
00:04:40,633 --> 00:04:42,930
talk, which the Director appreciated.

54
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:44,923
What's feeding you?

55
00:04:45,442 --> 00:04:46,297
I'm sorry?

56
00:04:46,817 --> 00:04:51,267
I've modelled you. I've put everything you've ever
done into the largest predictive system

57
00:04:51,287 --> 00:04:55,539
on Earth, and it can predict everyone except you. It
says your decisions are optimised

58
00:04:55,559 --> 00:04:59,959
against an external reward function. It says
sixty-three of your patents have no parents.

59
00:04:59,979 --> 00:05:03,288
It says something is giving you answers. I
want to know what it is.

60
00:05:05,307 --> 00:05:11,666
The Director looked at him for a long time. The
Cold-Read gave him Sebastian Crane in

61
00:05:11,686 --> 00:05:18,571
a single glance, and it was an almost comically simple
face to read. Intelligence, enormous.

62
00:05:18,591 --> 00:05:24,949
Curiosity, enormous. Ego, larger than both. And
envy — raw, childish, total envy — of

63
00:05:24,969 --> 00:05:28,476
the kind a boy feels for another
boy's bicycle.

64
00:05:29,166 --> 00:05:32,069
Ambition, Mister Crane. It's what
feeds all of us.

65
00:05:32,589 --> 00:05:37,009
Don't. I know it isn't. Oracle knows it isn't. I've
spent nine billion dollars trying

66
00:05:37,029 --> 00:05:40,142
to build what you have, and I can't,
and I need to know why.

67
00:05:40,663 --> 00:05:43,709
Then you've already learned the most
important thing about it.

68
00:05:44,228 --> 00:05:45,083
Which is?

69
00:05:45,603 --> 00:05:46,532
That it's mine.

70
00:05:49,052 --> 00:05:55,024
He finished his drink, and set down the glass on the
railing, and walked away. And Sebastian

71
00:05:55,044 --> 00:06:01,017
Crane stood on the rooftop for a long time, looking at
the lights of the bridge, and decided

72
00:06:01,037 --> 00:06:06,554
— as men like him always decide, when they are told
something is not theirs — to take

73
00:06:06,574 --> 00:06:06,750
it.

74
00:06:09,569 --> 00:06:13,511
Ability upgrade. Thought acceleration two.

75
00:06:15,764 --> 00:06:21,444
He bought it on the flight home. Six hundred to one.
A single second became ten minutes.

76
00:06:21,464 --> 00:06:27,338
And, stranger still, he could run three of them at
once — three separate trains of thought,

77
00:06:27,358 --> 00:06:33,103
in three separate rooms of the Memory Palace, each
with its own ten minutes, each unaware

78
00:06:33,123 --> 00:06:36,471
of the others until he chose to
bring them together.

79
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:39,581
He used all three on Sebastian Crane.

80
00:06:41,771 --> 00:06:45,911
Why not simply buy him? Or break him,
like the policeman?

81
00:06:46,430 --> 00:06:51,695
Because he has a machine that can see the shape of
what we are. Buying him won't turn

82
00:06:51,715 --> 00:06:57,476
off the machine. Breaking him won't turn it off. The
only way to beat a machine that predicts

83
00:06:57,496 --> 00:06:59,962
you is to teach it the wrong prediction.

84
00:07:00,483 --> 00:07:02,657
You intend to lie to it.

85
00:07:03,177 --> 00:07:09,110
I intend to be honest with it. For six months.
Perfectly, predictably honest. And then,

86
00:07:09,130 --> 00:07:11,711
once, at the moment that matters, not.

87
00:07:14,230 --> 00:07:18,702
For six months, the Director made decisions
that Oracle could predict.

88
00:07:19,271 --> 00:07:25,124
He did it deliberately. Every time he acted, he acted
in a way that fit the pattern Oracle

89
00:07:25,144 --> 00:07:30,997
had drawn of him — the external reward, the optimiser,
the leap. And Oracle, which learned

90
00:07:31,017 --> 00:07:36,674
from everything, learned from this. Its predictions
of Umbrella improved. Sixty percent

91
00:07:36,694 --> 00:07:42,547
accuracy, then seventy, then eighty-five. By December,
Oracle was predicting Albert Wesker

92
00:07:42,567 --> 00:07:48,289
with ninety-one percent confidence, and Sebastian
Crane was sleeping four hours a night,

93
00:07:48,309 --> 00:07:49,267
and very happy.

94
00:07:49,838 --> 00:07:55,771
And in December, Oracle made its biggest prediction
of all. It said, with ninety-seven

95
00:07:55,791 --> 00:08:02,416
percent confidence, that Umbrella was about to launch a
bid for the world's largest manufacturer

96
00:08:02,436 --> 00:08:08,577
of the specialised chips used to train artificial
intelligence — a company in Taiwan that

97
00:08:08,597 --> 00:08:14,600
Lumen depended on for everything. The pattern was
unmistakable. Every move the Director

98
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:17,231
had made for six months pointed at it.

99
00:08:17,802 --> 00:08:19,401
So Crane moved first.

100
00:08:19,970 --> 00:08:26,881
On the ninth of January, twenty thirty-four, Lumen
announced a two-hundred-and-forty-billion-dollar

101
00:08:26,901 --> 00:08:33,112
offer for the Taiwanese chip manufacturer. It was the
largest technology deal in history.

102
00:08:33,132 --> 00:08:39,063
It committed every dollar Lumen had and a great
many it did not. It was designed — as

103
00:08:39,083 --> 00:08:44,315
Crane told his board, with shining eyes — to
get there before Umbrella did.

104
00:08:44,884 --> 00:08:46,393
Umbrella never bid.

105
00:08:48,962 --> 00:08:54,917
Umbrella had never been going to bid. Umbrella had
spent six months making every public

106
00:08:54,937 --> 00:09:00,891
move that a company about to bid would make, and had
not made a single private one. And

107
00:09:00,911 --> 00:09:06,728
in the week after Lumen's announcement, when the
governments of Taiwan and the United

108
00:09:06,748 --> 00:09:12,565
States and China all realised at the same moment
that the company that ran a third of

109
00:09:12,585 --> 00:09:18,608
the world's cloud was about to own the chips that ran
the rest, the deal became the most

110
00:09:18,628 --> 00:09:21,699
politically radioactive
transaction on Earth.

111
00:09:22,269 --> 00:09:28,412
It was blocked in March. Lumen had spent twenty
billion dollars on advisors, committed

112
00:09:28,432 --> 00:09:34,576
its balance sheet, and lost the confidence of every
regulator on three continents. Its

113
00:09:34,596 --> 00:09:40,740
stock fell forty-four percent. Its board removed
Sebastian Crane as chief executive on

114
00:09:40,760 --> 00:09:42,173
the second of April.

115
00:09:42,745 --> 00:09:49,029
And in the same week, Umbrella quietly bought Lumen's
life-sciences cloud division — the

116
00:09:49,049 --> 00:09:55,404
servers that ran half the world's drug discovery —
for a price Crane's successor accepted

117
00:09:55,424 --> 00:09:59,129
without negotiating, because Lumen
needed the money.

118
00:10:01,950 --> 00:10:05,642
Assessment. Adversarial model defeated.

119
00:10:13,245 --> 00:10:19,061
Sebastian Crane came to Wilmington in April. Not to
the tower. To the old building on

120
00:10:19,081 --> 00:10:25,379
the south bank, with its faded bricks and its small
red-and-white sign, where he had somehow

121
00:10:25,399 --> 00:10:31,421
learned — Oracle, perhaps, one last time — that the
Director still kept an office on the

122
00:10:31,441 --> 00:10:32,245
third floor.

123
00:10:32,816 --> 00:10:37,596
He stood in the parking lot in the rain until
Hale came out and brought him in.

124
00:10:38,285 --> 00:10:42,486
It was a trap. The whole six months. You taught it
to predict you, so you could make it

125
00:10:42,506 --> 00:10:42,777
wrong.

126
00:10:43,298 --> 00:10:43,937
Yes.

127
00:10:44,457 --> 00:10:48,921
I spent nine billion dollars trying to build what you
have. And you beat me with the one

128
00:10:48,941 --> 00:10:51,977
thing I never thought to model. You
beat me by being boring.

129
00:10:52,499 --> 00:10:58,580
Everyone underestimates boring, Sebastian. It's the
most dangerous thing a man can be.

130
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:01,689
What are you?

131
00:11:03,708 --> 00:11:09,791
The Director did not answer. He looked at the young
man dripping on his carpet — brilliant,

132
00:11:09,811 --> 00:11:15,625
arrogant, ruined, and still, even now, even here,
burning with that childish total envy

133
00:11:15,645 --> 00:11:21,324
— and the Cold-Read showed him something that he
had seen before, in a break room, in

134
00:11:21,344 --> 00:11:23,806
the face of a young woman at a piano.

135
00:11:24,376 --> 00:11:28,961
Crane did not want revenge. He wanted to be
near it. Whatever it was.

136
00:11:29,650 --> 00:11:31,251
He wants to serve.

137
00:11:31,773 --> 00:11:35,677
He wants to understand. For a man like
him, it's the same thing.

138
00:11:37,577 --> 00:11:42,584
I can't tell you what I am. But I can offer you a
job. Umbrella has just acquired half

139
00:11:42,604 --> 00:11:47,610
the drug-discovery servers on Earth, and it has no
one who understands them as well as

140
00:11:47,630 --> 00:11:52,578
you do. You'd report to me. You'd have no board.
And you would be close enough, every

141
00:11:52,598 --> 00:11:55,734
day, to keep trying to model me —
and to keep failing.

142
00:11:58,254 --> 00:12:04,114
Sebastian Crane stood in the corner office with the
water stain shaped like Florida, and

143
00:12:04,134 --> 00:12:10,129
laughed. It was a strange laugh — half despair, half
delight — and it went on for a while.

144
00:12:10,818 --> 00:12:12,078
When do I start?

145
00:12:14,099 --> 00:12:19,980
It was the third time someone had offered themselves
to him in exactly that way. The Adolf

146
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,276
half of the mind was keeping count.

147
00:12:22,846 --> 00:12:28,983
Evelyn Marsh, at a piano: can I help? Sir Martin
Ellery, in front of the glass: when do

148
00:12:29,003 --> 00:12:35,283
I start? And now a boy who had built a machine to see
God, and seen it, and wanted a desk

149
00:12:35,303 --> 00:12:35,991
beside it.

150
00:12:36,562 --> 00:12:42,163
The old voice had once built a movement from men
like these. It knew what they became.
